{"records":[{"id":"mnrvyvt8bs","title":"Precise exogenous insertion and sequence replacements in poplar by simultaneous HDR overexpression and NHEJ suppression using CRISPR-Cas9","description":"CRISPR-mediated genome editing has become a powerful tool for the genetic modification of biological traits. However, developing an efficient, site-specific, gene knock-in system based on homology-directed DNA repair (HDR) remains a significant challenge in plants, especially in woody species like poplar. Here, we show that simultaneous inhibition of non-homologous end joining (NHEJ) recombination cofactor XRCC4 and overexpression of HDR enhancer factors CtIP and MRE11 can improve the HDR efficiency for gene knock-in. Using this approach, the BleoR gene was integrated onto the 3’ end of the MKK2 MAP Kinase gene to generate a BleoR-MKK2 fusion protein. Based on fully edited nucleotides evaluated by TaqMan real-time PCR, the HDR-mediated knock-in efficiency showed about 48% when using a XRCC4 silencing incorporated with a combination of CtIP and MRE11 overexpression compared to no HDR enhancement or NHEJ silencing. Furthermore, this corporation of HDR enhancer overexpression and NHEJ repression also increased genome targeting efficiency plus 7-fold fewer CRISPR-induced Insertions and Deletions (InDels), resulting in no functional effects on MKK2-based salt stress responses in poplar. Therefore, this approach may be useful not only in poplar and plants or crops but also in mammalians for improving CRISPR-mediated gene knock-in efficiency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-09T13:49:16.015Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mnrvyvt8bs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mnrvyvt8bs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ali","last_name":"Movahedi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hui ","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaohong ","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhong-Hua","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jake ","last_name":"C. Fountain","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhiying ","last_name":"Mu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Weibo","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiaxin ","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dawei ","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Baozhu ","last_name":"Guo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rajeev ","last_name":"K. Varshney","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Liming ","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiang","last_name":"Zhuge","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene Knockout","Polymorphic Analysis","CRISPR/Cas9","Genome Editing","Sanger Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ygpjshs8js","title":"Raw data from LACV whole genome CRISPR activation screen in BE(2)C cells by Francomacaro et al","description":"FASTQ files from control BE(2)C cells harboring whole-genome Calabrese CRISPR activation library (replicate 1)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-07T01:47:23.408Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ygpjshs8js","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ygpjshs8js"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"surachai","last_name":"supattapone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lisa","last_name":"Francomacaro","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01s5ya894","name":"National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke","grant_id":"R37NS125431"}],"categories":["Virus","Cell Death"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zmymsrxtkd","title":"Raw data from LACV whole genome CRISPR activation screen in BE(2)C cells by Francomacaro et al","description":"FASTQ files from control BE(2)C cells harboring whole-genome Calabrese CRISPR activation library (replicate 3)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-13T09:52:34.145Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zmymsrxtkd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zmymsrxtkd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"surachai","last_name":"supattapone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"LIsa","last_name":"Francomacaro","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01s5ya894","name":"National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke","grant_id":"R37NS125431"}],"categories":["Virus","Cell Death"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2gwd5xfmyh","title":"Raw data from LACV whole genome CRISPR activation screen in BE(2)C cells by Francomacaro et al","description":"\nFASTQ files from control BE(2)C cells harboring whole-genome Calabrese CRISPR activation library (replicate 2)\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-07T01:47:15.794Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2gwd5xfmyh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2gwd5xfmyh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"surachai","last_name":"supattapone","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01s5ya894","name":"National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke","grant_id":"R37NS125431"}],"categories":["Virus","Cell Death"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ptx3dzs926","title":"NGS data of CRISPR/dCas9 activation screen with CDK4/6 inhibitor Palbociclib in bladder cancer","description":"We used a genome-scale functional genomics approach to determine the mechanisms of resistance to PD0332991. Transcriptional activation was performed using the synergistic activation mediator (SAM) CRISPR-dCas9 library. Work flow: a) T24 cells with stably transduced SAM components. b) Low MOI lentiviral transduction of  amplified sgRNA library.  c)antibiotic selection for transduced  cells. e) divide cells into control(vehicle) and PD 7 days treatment. f) NGS of PCR amplicons. g) Comparison of sgRNA counts in control and treatment conditions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-06-01T04:24:44.789Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ptx3dzs926","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ptx3dzs926"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhichao","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8tpj5b47nn","title":"Raw data from LACV whole genome CRISPR activation screen in BE(2)C cells by Francomacaro et al","description":"FASTQ files from LACV-infected BE(2)C cells harboring whole-genome Calabrese CRISPR activation library (replicate 1)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-13T09:08:46.565Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8tpj5b47nn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8tpj5b47nn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"surachai","last_name":"supattapone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lisa","last_name":"Francomacaro","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01s5ya894","name":"National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke","grant_id":"R37NS125431"}],"categories":["Virus","Cell Death"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7hrhpv4y8d","title":"Raw data from LACV whole genome CRISPR activation screen in BE(2)C cells by Francomacaro et al","description":"FASTQ files from LACV-infected BE(2)C cells harboring whole-genome Calabrese CRISPR activation library (set C)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-07T01:46:58.21Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7hrhpv4y8d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7hrhpv4y8d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"surachai","last_name":"supattapone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lisa","last_name":"Francomacaro","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01s5ya894","name":"National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke","grant_id":"R37NS125431"}],"categories":["Virus","Cell Death"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"835fcfbvhs","title":"Preparation of CRISPR/Cas9 and gRNA plasmids","description":"Human genome engineering has been transformed by the introduction of the CRISPR (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats)/Cas (CRISPR-associated) system found in most bacteria and archaea. Type II CRISPR/Cas systems have been engineered to induce RNA-guided genome editing in human cells, where small RNAs function together with Cas9 nucleases for sequence-specific cleavage of target sequences. Here we describe the protocol for Cas9-mediated human genome engineering, including construct building and transfection methods necessary for delivering Cas9 and guide RNA (gRNA) into human-induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and HEK293 cells. Following genome editing, we also describe methods to assess genome editing effi- ciency using next-generation sequencing and isolate monoclonal hiPSCs with the desired modifications for downstream applications. Keywords: genome engineering • CRISPR • human stem cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2016-12-12T12:58:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/835fcfbvhs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/835fcfbvhs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Church","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Method"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9b83hvkgm5","title":"Balancing cohesin eviction and retention prevents aberrant chromosomal interactions, Polycomb-mediated repression, and X inactivation","description":"Western blot and immunofluorescence images for Figures 1 and S1\n\nDepletion of architectural factors globally alters chromatin structure but only modestly affects gene expression. We revisit the structure-function relationship using the inactive X chromosome (Xi) as a model. We investigate cohesin imbalances by forcing its depletion or retention using degron-tagged RAD21 (cohesin subunit) or WAPL (cohesin release factor). Cohesin loss disrupts the Xi superstructure, unveiling superloops between escapee genes with minimal effect on gene repression. By contrast, forced cohesin retention markedly affects Xi superstructure, compromises spreading of Xist RNA-Polycomb complexes, and attenuates Xi silencing. Effects are greatest at distal chromosomal ends, where looping contacts with the Xist locus are weakened. Surprisingly, cohesin loss creates an Xi superloop, and cohesin retention creates Xi megadomains on the active X chromosome. Across the genome, a proper cohesin balance protects against aberrant inter-chromosomal interactions and tempers Polycomb-mediated repression. We conclude that a balance of cohesin eviction and retention regulates X inactivation and inter-chromosomal interactions across the genome.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-04T21:07:37.24Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9b83hvkgm5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9b83hvkgm5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrea","last_name":"Kriz","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3ctpx4k6jp","title":"Porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome virus upregulates SMPDL3B to promote viral replication by modulating lipid metabolism","description":"We generated a collection of porcine genome-scale CRISPR/Cas9 knockout (PigGeCKO) cells on a PAM background.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-26T18:53:54.241Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3ctpx4k6jp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3ctpx4k6jp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"huanh","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Agricultural Biotechnology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":138566,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_tim